 #include <stdlib.h> 
 #include <omp.h> 
 #include <stdio.h> 
 #define THREADS 16 
 #define N 100000000 
 int main ( ) { 
 int i; 
 printf("Running %d iterations on %d threads dynamically.
", N, THREADS); 
 #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) num_threads(THREADS) 
 for (i = 0; i < N; i++) { 
 /* a loop that doesn't take very long */ } 
 /* all threads done */ 
 printf("All done!
"); 
 return 0; }
What is the performance issue in the given code snippet? 
Correct Answer: Dynamic scheduling is better when the iterations may take very different amounts of time. However, there is some overhead to dynamic scheduling. After each iteration, the threads must stop and receive a new value of the loop variable to use for its next iteration.

